    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the concept.\n\n**Stored XSS incident** is a production-security response topic. Explain detection, containment, evidence preservation, remediation, recovery, and prevention without destroying forensic context.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the protected asset, attacker capability, trust boundary, secure default, enforcement point, and recovery path before writing code.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core policy or state check so you can explain the security reasoning line by line during the interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface Incident {\n  severity: \"low\" | \"medium\" | \"high\";\n  contained: boolean;\n}\n\nfunction needsContainment(\n  incident: Incident,\n): boolean {\n  return (\n    incident.severity === \"high\" &&\n    !incident.contained\n  );\n}\n\nconst required = needsContainment({\n  severity: \"high\",\n  contained: false,\n});\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or browser APIs when they make the same control clearer without hiding the security boundary.\n\n```ts\nconst incident = {\n  severity: \"high\" as const,\n  contained: false,\n};\n\nconst required =\n  incident.severity === \"high\" &&\n  !incident.contained;\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse Stored XSS incident during production response to preserve evidence, reduce attacker access, restore safe service, and add a regression control after remediation.\n\nStep 6 — Validation:\nTest the intended path, denied path, malformed input, missing/expired state, misconfiguration, and recovery/rollback behavior where applicable.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what is trusted, what is untrusted, where final enforcement occurs, why the default is safe, and how monitoring or testing prevents recurrence.",
