    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the concept.\n\n**Clickjacking incident** is a browser/UI security boundary problem. The key is controlling framing, opener relationships, user interaction, and which origins are allowed to embed or navigate the application.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the protected asset, attacker capability, trust boundary, secure default, enforcement point, and recovery path before writing code.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core policy or state check so you can explain the security reasoning line by line during the interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface FramePolicy {\n  allowedParents: readonly string[];\n}\n\nfunction canEmbed(\n  policy: FramePolicy,\n  origin: string,\n): boolean {\n  for (let i = 0; i < policy.allowedParents.length; i += 1) {\n    if (policy.allowedParents[i] === origin) return true;\n  }\n\n  return false;\n}\n\nconst allowed = canEmbed(\n  { allowedParents: [\"https://portal.example.com\"] },\n  \"https://portal.example.com\",\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or browser APIs when they make the same control clearer without hiding the security boundary.\n\n```ts\nconst allowedParents = new Set([\n  \"https://portal.example.com\",\n]);\n\nconst allowed =\n  allowedParents.has(\"https://portal.example.com\");\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse Clickjacking incident at the browser framing/navigation boundary. Keep embedding permissions narrow and verify response headers and window relationships in browser tests.\n\nStep 6 — Validation:\nTest the intended path, denied path, malformed input, missing/expired state, misconfiguration, and recovery/rollback behavior where applicable.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what is trusted, what is untrusted, where final enforcement occurs, why the default is safe, and how monitoring or testing prevents recurrence.",
    productionExample: "Use Clickjacking incident at the browser framing/navigation boundary. Keep embedding permissions narrow and verify response headers and window relationships in browser tests.",
